From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Scott Quarrier <squarrier@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 00:21:59 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] glm5_next/cuda: knobs for the g53 expert kernels'
 configuration (TF_GLM_EXPERT_KERNEL_MT, _GW, _NT, _L2, _FUSE, _DISCARD, _SK,
 _SKD, _SWIGLU)

Under TF_GLM_EXPERT_KERNEL=g53 (glm53f-afd's large-M expert prompt kernels, written by Hugh Madden, glm53f-afd @
91db3cc), every field of the kernels' configuration (glm53f-rank's g53r_cfg) now has an env knob on the expert
containers, so the launch shape can be tuned for the GPU the experts run on. The defaults were measured on another
GPU, at four ranks of half this width.

- Unset (every knob): nothing changes. switch() returns the settings it returned before, every window runs
  default_cfg as before, the node calls routed() as before and prints the same line.
- Set: one value for every window size. MT (2, 4: 32- or 64-row groups), GW (8, 16 gate/up warps), NT (1, 2, 4:
  down chunks of 128 nt columns), L2 (1, 2: evict-first trellis loads), FUSE and DISCARD (1, 2) keep every bit
  (glm53f-rank's own statement; a CPU emulation of the kernels' work decomposition shows it, and a GPU test checks
  the CUDA kernels' bytes at 256-2,048 rows). SK, SKD (1, 2, 4) and SWIGLU (0, 1) change outputs; set, they too
  apply to every window, so a prompt row's bits still never depend on its window. A K split that needs more than
  the node's prompt scratch gets its buffer at the node's start, and the line says how much.
- The node's line adds "schedule <fields> for every window (the same bits | <fields>: outputs change)" when a knob
  is set.

Tests: tests/test_glm_g53_schedule.py (CPU: names, refusals, the defaults unchanged at every window size, one field a
knob, the node's calls and line; and tests/glm_g53_schedule.py's emulated digests: the schedule fields give the
default's bits, sk, skd and the SwiGLU precision other bits, the same under every schedule, a row's bits whatever
its window, and a negative control). tests/cuda/test_glm_g53_kernel.py gains the schedules' byte check and a times
report (TF_G53_TUNE_TIMES) for the GPU. tests/test_glm_g53_kernel.py clears the knobs too, so a run's environment
cannot move its switch tests.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 (planning and execution), via Hermes

